# TOON v3.0 – Feature Specification
# .agent/specs/features/duress-session-routing.spec.toon
# Migrated from docs/specs/DG-001-duress-session-routing (.md + .toon). Legacy id: DG-001.
# Plan (human doc): .agent/plans/duress-session-routing.md
# Acceptance: .agent/specs/acceptance/duress-session-routing.acceptance.toon

feature:
  id: FT-001
  legacy-id: DG-001
  name: Duress session routing
  version: 0.1.0-SNAPSHOT
  status: implemented
  owner: Felipe Martins

modules[3]: decoy-guard-core,decoy-guard-spring-boot-starter,decoy-guard-demo

stack:
  java: 21
  spring_boot: 4.1.2
  build: maven

requirement:
  summary: A coerced user must be able to log in and see a believable account and "send" transfers without any real effect while the bank is notified. Each call on a routed service reaches the real or the decoy implementation per session and transparently so application code (controllers and use cases) never needs to know whether the session is real or a decoy.
  actor: Account holder under coercion (logs in with the duress credential) and the bank application code that calls routed services
  trigger: DuressActivator.activate(sessionId trigger) at login then every HTTP request carrying the session id

goals[4]:
  Route each call to the real or the decoy implementation of a service per session and transparently
  Keep decoy implementations impossible to inject by mistake
  Raise exactly one silent alert when a session enters duress mode
  Keep every storage and alert component replaceable

non-goals[3]:
  Risk signal detection on the device (future spec)
  Durable storage implementations (future spec)
  Response time shaping (future spec)

inputs[5]{name,type,required,constraints}:
  sessionId,String,false,Opaque session id from SessionIdExtractor (default: bearer token). Null means anonymous and NORMAL
  trigger,ActivationTrigger,true,DURESS_CREDENTIAL or RISK_SIGNAL or MANUAL. Required by DuressActivator.activate
  routed interface call,method invocation,true,Any method of an interface that has a @Decoy implementation
  @Decoy.value,Class[],false,Empty means every interface the bean implements. Listed types must be interfaces the bean implements
  decoy-guard.enabled,boolean,false,Defaults to true

outputs:
  success:
    description: The call is served by the real bean in a NORMAL session and by the @Decoy bean in a DURESS session. Responses have the same shape in both modes. The first activation of a session publishes one SilentAlert.
    status: 200
    body[3]{name,type,value}:
      DuressContext,record,sessionId mode trigger activatedAt
      SilentAlert,record,sessionId trigger raisedAt
      HeldTransaction,record,id sessionId type amount counterparty heldAt
  errors[2]{code,status,when}:
    INSUFFICIENT_FUNDS,422,Pix amount above the available balance. Same status and message ("Saldo insuficiente") in both modes
    UNAUTHORIZED,401,Wrong PIN at login or no open session on /account. Same in both modes

startup-failures[2]{code,when}:
  DUPLICATE_DECOY,Two @Decoy beans stand in for the same interface
  INVALID_DECOY_INTERFACE,@Decoy lists a type that the bean does not implement as an interface

business-rules[10]{id,source,priority,rule}:
  BR-001,FR-01,must,A session has a mode NORMAL or DURESS. Unknown or anonymous sessions are NORMAL
  BR-002,FR-02,must,DuressActivator.activate(sessionId trigger) switches a session to DURESS and is idempotent. The first activation's trigger and time are kept
  BR-003,FR-03,must,Each activation of a previously NORMAL session publishes exactly one SilentAlert
  BR-004,FR-04,must,A bean annotated @Decoy stands in for the interfaces it implements or only those listed in the annotation
  BR-005,FR-05,must,Beans implementing a routed interface are wrapped so that per call DURESS sessions reach the decoy and NORMAL sessions reach the real bean
  BR-006,FR-06,must,@Decoy beans are never autowire candidates including when the starter is disabled
  BR-007,FR-07,must,Two @Decoy beans for the same interface fail application startup
  BR-008,FR-08,must,The session mode is resolved once per HTTP request and cleared at the end of it
  BR-009,FR-09,should,Decoy transfers are recorded as HeldTransactions with the destination typed by the user
  BR-010,FR-10,should,decoy-guard.enabled=false disables routing and the filter and the default SPI beans

non-functional[4]{id,requirement}:
  NFR-01,The mode is never written to any response (headers or body or cookies)
  NFR-02,A failing alert channel never fails the request that triggered it
  NFR-03,decoy-guard-core has no runtime dependencies
  NFR-04,Java 21 and Spring Boot 4.1

components[7]{name,module,role}:
  DuressContext,core,Record with sessionId and mode and trigger and activatedAt
  DuressContextHolder,core,ThreadLocal binding of the current context
  DuressActivator,core,Activates the session and publishes one alert swallowing alert failures
  DuressContextFilter,starter,Servlet filter (order HIGHEST_PRECEDENCE + 100) resolving the context per request
  DecoyRoutingPostProcessor,starter,BFPP hides decoys and maps interface to decoy. BPP wraps real beans in routing JDK proxies
  SessionIdExtractor,starter,SPI. Default reads the bearer token
  DecoyGuardAutoConfiguration,starter,Wires defaults with ConditionalOnMissingBean

spis[4]{name,default,production_hint}:
  DuressSessionRegistry,InMemoryDuressSessionRegistry,Redis or database shared by all instances
  HeldTransactionStore,InMemoryHeldTransactionStore,Durable store read by the fraud team
  SilentAlertPublisher,LoggingSilentAlertPublisher,Fraud queue or trusted contact channel
  SessionIdExtractor,BearerTokenSessionIdExtractor,JWT claim or session cookie

rules[4]:
  Inject routed services by interface and never by concrete class
  Real and decoy must share DTOs and exceptions and id formats
  Never expose the session mode to the client
  The decoy must keep working when the alert channel fails

assumptions[3]:
  Business rule ids BR-001 to BR-010 map one to one to the legacy functional requirements FR-01 to FR-10 (column source)
  Error codes INSUFFICIENT_FUNDS and UNAUTHORIZED name the demo behavior covered by AC-04 and DuressFlowTest. The legacy spec had no error codes
  Startup failures are not HTTP errors so they are listed in startup-failures and not in outputs.errors
